window.METHODS=[
['translate','1.1','Translate a sentence','if · only if · necessary · sufficient · unless','Name atomic propositions.|Find the main connective.|Translate only-if as → toward the requirement.|Check the false case: hypothesis true, conclusion false.','p only if q: p → q','Do not turn a sufficient condition into an iff.'],
['truth','1.2','Build a truth table','assignment · tautology · contradiction · satisfiable','List 2ⁿ assignments in a fixed order.|Evaluate inner subformulas before outer ones.|Compare complete columns.|Classify all-true, all-false or mixed.','p → q ≡ ¬p ∨ q','A contradiction is unsatisfiable. A contingency is satisfiable.'],
['equivalence','1.3','Prove an equivalence','simplify · logical laws · same truth values','Eliminate → and ↔ if helpful.|Push negations inward with De Morgan.|Use one named law per step.|Finish at the target, or compare all assignments.','¬(p → q) ≡ p ∧ ¬q','A few matching truth assignments do not prove equivalence.'],
['proof','1.4','Write a propositional proof','premises · conclusion · validity · inference','List and number the premises.|Inspect the desired conclusion and needed intermediate claims.|Apply MP, MT, HS, DS, conjunction, simplification or resolution.|Cite both the rule and the line numbers.','p → q, ¬q ⊢ ¬p','Affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent are invalid.'],
['quantify','2.2','Choose quantifier order','every · some · one for all · depends on','State the domain and predicate meanings.|For all restricted objects use implication.|For some restricted object use conjunction.|Read quantifiers left to right and track witness dependency.','∀x∃y(x+y=0): y may depend on x','One witness per row differs from one common column.'],
['negate','2.3','Negate a quantified formula','not every · none · counterexample','Keep the domain and the quantifier order.|Swap each quantifier as ¬ passes it.|Apply De Morgan to the body.|Replace ¬(P→Q) with P∧¬Q.','¬∀x∃y P ≡ ∃x∀y ¬P','Negating ∀ to ∃ without negating the body is incomplete.'],
['predicate-proof','2.4','Use a fresh witness','UI · EI · EG · UG · predicate proof','Instantiate an existential with a fresh name.|Instantiate universals at that same witness.|Apply propositional rules to the instantiated facts.|Use EG; use UG only for an arbitrary object.','∀x(F→G), ∃x(F∧H) ⊢ ∃x(G∧H)','An existential witness cannot be treated as arbitrary for UG.'],
['sets','3.2','Prove a set identity','membership · complement · subset · Venn','Fix the same universal set.|Rewrite differences as intersection with complements.|Use arbitrary-element membership equivalences or a named law chain.|For equality prove both directions; for a false claim give a small counterexample.','(A∪B)ᶜ = Aᶜ∩Bᶜ','Membership ∈ and subset ⊆ refer to different relationships.'],
['count','3.3','Fill an inclusion–exclusion diagram','at least one · none · exactly two · survey','Write universe and all intersection counts.|Put the triple in the center.|Subtract the triple from each pair.|Subtract the filled overlaps from each single set.|Add requested disjoint regions and check the universe.','Union = singles − pairs + triple','Pair-intersection counts include the triple unless stated otherwise.'],
['compose','4.2','Compose relations','Boolean product · R∘S · R² · inverse','State the row and column order.|Follow first R then S, matching the middle coordinate.|Use AND inside each route and OR across routes.|Deduplicate endpoint pairs or keep a 0–1 matrix.','M(R∘S) = M(R) ⊙ M(S)','The course uses left-to-right composition. Ordinary products count routes instead.'],
['properties','4.3','Classify a relation','reflexive · symmetric · antisymmetric · transitive','Inspect all diagonal entries.|Compare each off-diagonal pair with its reverse.|Separate symmetric, antisymmetric and asymmetric tests.|Check every two-step route for its required shortcut.|Give explicit witnesses for failures.','Transitive ⇔ M⊙M ≤ M','Antisymmetric does not mean not symmetric.'],
['closure','4.4','Find the smallest closure','reflexive closure · symmetric closure · reachability · Warshall','Start with every original pair.|Add loops for reflexivity or reversed pairs for symmetry.|For transitivity run Warshall with k outermost.|Check positive return paths before adding transitive loops.','Wᵢⱼ ← Wᵢⱼ ∨ (Wᵢₖ ∧ Wₖⱼ)','R⁺ and reflexive-transitive R* differ by automatic identity pairs.'],
['classes','4.5','Find equivalence classes','modulo · partition · quotient set','Prove reflexive, symmetric and transitive.|Collect the class of each unused element.|Remove duplicate classes.|Verify blocks are nonempty, disjoint and cover the domain.|Build R by union of each block squared if needed.','|R| = Σ block-size²','A/R is a set of sets; its size is the number of distinct classes.'],
['hasse','4.6','Read a Hasse diagram','maximal · greatest · upper bound · lub · glb','Write the ambient poset and queried subset.|Recover comparisons by upward paths and equality.|Find subset extrema within that subset.|List common bounds in the ambient set.|Compare bounds to select lub or glb.','Bounds may lie outside the queried subset.','A maximal element need not dominate all other elements.'],
['degree','5.1','Count degrees and edges','loops · handshaking · complete · complement','Identify undirected or directed edges.|Count an undirected loop twice, a directed loop once in and once out.|Check Σd=2m or Σin=Σout=m.|Use n(n−1)/2 only for simple graphs.','Σd(v)=2m','A crossing is not a vertex unless the graph marks it as a junction.'],
['walks','5.3','Count walks with matrices','length k · adjacency power · subgraph · connectivity','State vertex order and adjacency edge counts.|Use ordinary arithmetic multiplication.|Compute the kth power.|Read entry (start,end).|Allow repetitions unless the problem specifies a simple path.','Number of length-k walks i→j = (Aᵏ)ᵢⱼ','Boolean multiplication answers existence, not count.'],
['euler','5.4','Decide Euler or Hamilton','one stroke · every edge · every vertex · circuit','Choose edges-once (Euler) or vertices-once (Hamilton).|For Euler first check connectivity of edge-bearing vertices.|Count odd degrees: 0 closed, 2 open, more neither.|For Hamilton try an explicit cycle, an obstruction, or Ore/Dirac with all assumptions.|Give a traversal if construction is requested.','Euler: 0 or 2 odd; Hamilton: different tests.','Failure of Ore or Dirac does not prove non-Hamiltonian.'],
['algebra','6.1','Read an operation table','identity · inverse · associative · group · idempotent','Check every output remains in the set.|Compare mirrored cells for commutativity.|Find a row and column both equal to headers.|Locate two-sided inverses relative to that identity.|Check all triples for associativity or prove it by representation.','Identity e: e*a = a*e = a','A Latin square alone does not prove associativity.'],
['ring','6.3','Classify a structure','semigroup · group · ring · field','State the set and operations.|For a group check associativity, identity and all inverses.|For a ring check the additive abelian group, associative multiplication and both distributive laws.|For a field require the nonzero multiplicative abelian group.','Z is a ring; Q is a field.','Zero has no multiplicative inverse in a field.'],
['boolean','6.4','Simplify Boolean algebra','absorption · distributive · complement · duality','Identify + as OR, · as AND, and the complement.|Factor or distribute one step at a time.|Use complement and identity laws.|Check truth values or the set interpretation.','a·b + a·b̄ = a','Boolean OR: 1+1=1. Mod2 field addition: 1⊕1=0.']
].map(([id,topic,title,cue,steps,formula,trap])=>({id,topic,chapter:+topic[0],title,cue,steps:steps.split('|'),formula,trap}));
// Each question has an authored explanation, not only an answer key.
window.QUESTIONS=[
['1.1','Which assignment makes p → q false?',['p=0,q=0','p=0,q=1','p=1,q=0','p=1,q=1'],2,'Only a true hypothesis followed by a false conclusion violates material implication.'],
['1.1','“p only if q” means:',['q → p','p → q','p ↔ q','¬p → q'],1,'q is a necessary condition for p. When p holds, q must hold.'],
['1.1','The contrapositive of p → q is:',['q → p','¬p → ¬q','¬q → ¬p','p → ¬q'],2,'Reverse the implication and negate both parts. The contrapositive is equivalent to the original.'],
['1.1','Exclusive OR is true when:',['Both inputs true','Both inputs false','At least one input true','Exactly one input true'],3,'Inclusive OR allows both. Exclusive OR excludes that case.'],
['1.2','How many rows for four distinct propositional variables?',16,'Each variable has two choices, giving 2⁴=16 assignments.'],
['1.2','p ∧ ¬p is:',['A tautology','A satisfiable contingency','An unsatisfiable contradiction','Not a WFF'],2,'No assignment makes both p and ¬p true, although the expression is well formed.'],
['1.2','A tautology is satisfiable.',['True','False'],0,'Satisfiable means at least one true assignment; a tautology is true on every assignment.'],
['1.3','¬(p ∨ q) is equivalent to:',['¬p ∨ ¬q','¬p ∧ ¬q','p ∧ q','p → q'],1,'De Morgan swaps OR for AND while negating both inputs.'],
['1.3','¬(p → q) is equivalent to:',['¬p ∧ q','p ∧ ¬q','¬p ∨ q','p ↔ q'],1,'Negating an implication selects its unique failure condition: true antecedent and false consequent.'],
['1.3','p ∨ (p ∧ q) simplifies to:',['p','q','p ∧ q','p ∨ q'],0,'Absorption: the smaller intersection adds nothing to the already included p cases.'],
['1.4','From p → q and ¬q infer:',['p','q','¬p','Nothing'],2,'Modus tollens: if p held it would force q, contradicting ¬q.'],
['1.4','From p → q and q, inferring p is:',['Modus ponens','Modus tollens','Valid simplification','Invalid affirming the consequent'],3,'q might hold for another reason. p=0,q=1 makes both premises true and the conclusion false.'],
['1.4','Resolve p∨q with ¬p∨r to obtain:',['q∨r','p∨r','q∧r','¬q'],0,'Resolution eliminates the complementary p and ¬p pair, leaving q∨r.'],
['2.1','All comedians are funny, over all people:',['∀x(Cx∧Fx)','∀x(Cx→Fx)','∃x(Cx→Fx)','∃x(Cx∧Fx)'],1,'Universal restriction uses implication; it does not assert that noncomedians are comedians.'],
['2.1','To disprove ∀x P(x), you need:',['One witness to P','One counterexample to P','All elements satisfy P','A fresh arbitrary symbol only'],1,'A single domain element failing P is enough to refute the universal claim.'],
['2.2','Over R, ∀x∃y(x+y=0) is:',['True: choose y=−x','False: y must be fixed','True: choose y=0 for every x','False: x=1 is impossible'],0,'The existential occurs after ∀x, so y may depend on x.'],
['2.2','Over R, ∃y∀x(x+y=0) is:',['True','False'],1,'x=0 forces y=0, while x=1 forces y=−1; no fixed y works for all x.'],
['2.2','In ∃x P(x) ∧ Q(x), without outer parentheses, x in Q(x) is:',['Bound by ∃x','Free','A constant','Not allowed in any WFF'],1,'The quantifier covers only its immediate formula P(x). A WFF may have free variables.'],
['2.2','For P(x,y):xy=0 over R, ∃x∀y P(x,y) is:',['True: x=0 works','False: y=1 fails for every x','True only if y=0','Not a closed formula'],0,'Choose the single witness x=0. Then xy=0 for every real y.'],
['2.3','Negate ∀x∃y P(x,y):',['∀x∀y ¬P','∃x∀y ¬P','∃y∀x ¬P','∃x∃y P'],1,'Swap each quantifier while preserving its order, then negate the body.'],
['2.3','“Not every student passed” means:',['No student passed','Every student failed','Some student did not pass','Some student passed'],2,'A universal fails with one counterexample, even if all remaining students passed.'],
['2.4','EI from ∃x P(x) permits:',['P(Alice) for an existing named person','P(c) with c fresh as an unspecified witness','∀x P(x)','P(c) for every c'],1,'The fresh name refers to some witness; it does not identify that witness with a preselected object.'],
['2.4','You may universally generalize from an existential witness.',['True','False'],1,'The witness may have special properties. UG requires an arbitrary object, not merely some object.'],
['3.1','How many elements does P({a,b,c}) have?',8,'A three-element set has 2³=8 subsets.'],
['3.1','How many elements does {∅} have?',1,'Its single element is the empty set. Braces turn that object into a nonempty set.'],
['3.1','P(∅) equals:',['∅','{∅}','{{∅}}','0'],1,'The empty set has exactly one subset, namely itself.'],
['3.1','x∈P(A) means:',['x∈A','x⊆A','A⊆x','x=A'],1,'The elements of a power set are precisely the subsets of its underlying set.'],
['3.2','A∖B=∅ is equivalent to:',['A=B','B=∅','A⊆B','A∩B=∅'],2,'No element of A lies outside B. B can still contain additional elements.'],
['3.2','If A={1,2,3}, B={3,4}, what is |A△B|?',3,'The symmetric difference is {1,2,4}; the common element 3 is excluded.'],
['3.2','A∖B=A forces B=∅.',['True','False'],1,'A={1}, B={2} are disjoint and nonempty, yet A∖B=A.'],
['3.3','48 students; football15, basketball25, both8. Neither?',16,'The union is 15+25−8=32, leaving 48−32=16.'],
['3.3','Source survey: 272 total, union154. None?',118,'Subtract the union from the universe: 272−154=118. The exactly-one count106 is a different region total.'],
['3.3','Three-set pair intersections sum to76 and triple is14. Exactly two?',34,'Pair totals count each triple member three times. Subtract 3·14: 76−42=34.'],
['4.1','|A|=3, |B|=2. How many relations from A to B?',64,'There are 6 possible ordered pairs and 2⁶ possible subsets.'],
['4.1','Course R∘S means:',['First S then R','First R then S','R∪S','R∩S'],1,'The course reads relation composition left to right. Match this convention before multiplying matrices.'],
['4.2','Boolean dot product of [1,1,0] and [1,1,0]ᵀ is:',1,'(1∧1)∨(1∧1)∨(0∧0)=1. Ordinary arithmetic would give2, but Boolean multiplication records existence.'],
['4.2','The matrix of an inverse relation is:',['The arithmetic matrix inverse','The transpose','The complement','Always the identity'],1,'Reverse every ordered pair, exchanging its row and column indices. This is unrelated to numerical matrix inversion.'],
['4.3','The identity relation on a nonempty set is:',['Symmetric only','Antisymmetric only','Both symmetric and antisymmetric','Asymmetric'],2,'Loops reverse to themselves, and no distinct elements form a reciprocal pair.'],
['4.3','R={(1,2),(2,1)} on {1,2} is transitive.',['True','False'],1,'1R2 and 2R1 require1R1; similarly2R2. Both loops are missing.'],
['4.3','An asymmetric relation can have a loop.',['True','False'],1,'A loop is its own reverse, directly violating asymmetry.'],
['4.4','Does R⁺ always include every identity pair?',['Yes','No'],1,'Only existing loops or positive-length return walks force loops in R⁺. R* adds all identity pairs automatically.'],
['4.4','R={1→2,2→3} on {1,2,3}. Transitive closure adds:',['1→3 only','All three loops','3→1 only','Nothing'],0,'The two-step route1→2→3 forces1→3, and there are no cycles forcing loops.'],
['4.4','Warshall requires which loop outermost?',['i','j','k, the allowed intermediate vertex','Any ordering always works'],2,'Stage k must incorporate all routes through the kth allowed intermediate vertex before advancing stages.'],
['4.5','On {1,…,8}, congruence mod3 gives how many distinct classes?',3,'The blocks are {1,4,7}, {2,5,8}, {3,6}.'],
['4.5','A partition has block sizes3,3,2. Its induced relation has how many pairs?',22,'Every ordered pair within each block belongs: 3²+3²+2²=22.'],
['4.5','Two equivalence classes overlap. They must be:',['Equal','Disjoint','Singletons','Invalid regardless of relation'],0,'Equivalence classes are equal or disjoint. One shared element forces all members of either class into the other.'],
['4.6','Under divisibility on {2,4,5,10,12,20,25}, greatest element is:',['25','20','12','None'],3,'No member is divisible by every other member. There are three maximal elements, not one greatest.'],
['4.6','In ambient {1,…,12} under divisibility, lub({2,4,6}) is:',12,'The common multiples inside the ambient set consist only of12. The bound need not belong to the queried subset.'],
['4.6','In a Hasse diagram, every transitive shortcut is drawn.',['True','False'],1,'Hasse diagrams keep only covers. All removed comparisons are recovered by upward paths and equality.'],
['5.1','An undirected graph has degree sum20. How many edges?',10,'Every edge contributes two degree units, including a loop with both ends at one vertex.'],
['5.1','How many edges does K₆ have?',15,'n(n−1)/2=6·5/2=15 distinct unordered pairs.'],
['5.1','One directed loop contributes:',['2 in and0 out','0 in and2 out','1 in and1 out','0 to both'],2,'The loop leaves and arrives at the same vertex once.'],
['5.2','Course signed incidence uses +1 for:',['The terminal vertex','The initial vertex','Every nonincident vertex','A loop only'],1,'The course convention is +1 at the tail and −1 at the head; some texts use the reverse.'],
['5.2','An incidence matrix for n vertices and m edges has dimensions:',['n×n','m×m','n×m','m×n in the course'],2,'Rows index vertices, columns index labelled edges.'],
['5.3','(Aᵏ)ᵢⱼ using ordinary arithmetic counts:',['Simple paths only','Length-k walks','Connected components','Euler circuits'],1,'Walks may repeat vertices and edges. Boolean powers instead record whether at least one walk exists.'],
['5.3','A spanning subgraph must keep:',['All edges','All vertices','Connectivity','The same degrees'],1,'Spanning means V′=V. Edges may be removed and the result can be disconnected.'],
['5.4','Connected undirected graph, exactly two odd vertices:',['Euler circuit','Open Euler trail only','Neither Euler traversal','Hamilton circuit guaranteed'],1,'The odd vertices must be the two endpoints. A closed Euler tour requires all degrees even.'],
['5.4','Failing Dirac’s condition proves no Hamilton circuit.',['True','False'],1,'Dirac is sufficient, not necessary. A five-cycle is Hamiltonian while all degrees2 are below2.5.'],
['5.4','A graph has three leaves. Can it have a Hamilton path?',['Yes always','No','Only if all degrees even','Only if complete'],1,'Every leaf must be an endpoint of a spanning simple path, but a path has only two endpoints.'],
['6.1','In the course table a,b,c,d, identity a and c*d=d*c=a. Inverse of c is:',['a','b','c','d'],3,'An inverse produces the identity in both multiplication orders.'],
['6.1','A symmetric Cayley table proves associativity.',['True','False'],1,'Symmetry proves commutativity. Associativity must compare all triples or follow from a valid general proof.'],
['6.2','(Z⁺,+) is a group.',['True','False'],1,'It is associative and closed, hence a semigroup, but its additive identity0 and negatives are absent.'],
['6.3','(Z,+,×) is:',['A field only','A ring and field','A ring but not field','Neither'],2,'Addition gives an abelian group and multiplication distributes, but2 has no integer multiplicative inverse.'],
['6.3','Multiplicative inverse of2 modulo5 is:',3,'2·3=6≡1 mod5. The inverse must give the multiplicative identity, not0.'],
['6.3','Why is Z₆ not a field?',['Addition is not commutative','2·3=0 mod6, so nonzero elements need not be invertible','It has no0','It is not a ring'],1,'A field cannot have nonzero zero divisors. In Z₆,2 and3 are nonzero but their product vanishes.'],
['6.4','In Boolean algebra, 1+1 equals:',1,'Here + is OR, so1 OR1=1. It is not the field addition of Z₂.'],
['6.4','a·b+a·b̄ simplifies to:',['a','b','0','1'],0,'Factor a, then use b+b̄=1 and a·1=a.']
].map((q,i)=>typeof q[2]==='number'?{id:'q'+i,topic:q[0],chapter:+q[0][0],prompt:q[1],answer:q[2],explanation:q[3],type:'number'}:{id:'q'+i,topic:q[0],chapter:+q[0][0],prompt:q[1],choices:q[2],answer:q[3],explanation:q[4],type:'choice'});
